            "The International Conference on Population and Development held in Cairo in 1994 gave the field its working definition: reproductive health is a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being in all matters relating to the reproductive system, not merely the absence of disease, and it implies that people are able to have a satisfying and safe sex life, the capability to reproduce and the freedom to decide if, when and how often to do so. <strong>Sexual and reproductive health and rights (SRHR)</strong> therefore joins a set of services to a set of entitlements. The services are those this course covers: contraception and family planning; safe pregnancy and birth, which the obstetric course covered and this one touches only where it meets gynaecology; the prevention and treatment of sexually transmitted infections including HIV; the prevention and management of unsafe abortion and post-abortion care; the detection and treatment of cancers of the cervix, breast, ovary, uterus and prostate; the investigation and treatment of infertility; the care of adolescents; the response to sexual and gender-based violence and to harmful practices such as female genital mutilation and child marriage; the health of women after the reproductive years; and the sexual health of men. The entitlements are the right to information, to privacy and confidentiality, to choose a method or decline one, to consent, to be treated with dignity whatever one's age, marital status, disability or diagnosis, and to services that actually exist within reach. The Sustainable Development Goals write this into targets 3.7 (universal access to sexual and reproductive health services) and 5.6 (universal access to sexual and reproductive health and reproductive rights), and Ghana reports against both.",
