            "Almost everything in this course — the cycle, the pill, the implant, infertility, the hot flush — can be reasoned from one chain of command. The <strong>hypothalamus</strong> releases gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) in pulses; the <strong>anterior pituitary</strong> answers with follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinising hormone (LH); and the <strong>gonad</strong> — ovary or testis — makes the sex steroids and the gametes. In a woman FSH recruits and ripens a cohort of follicles, the dominant follicle makes rising <strong>oestrogen</strong>, which rebuilds the endometrium and, when it stays high for about two days, flips its feedback from negative to positive and triggers the <strong>LH surge</strong>; ovulation follows some 24 to 36 hours later, the ruptured follicle becomes the <strong>corpus luteum</strong> and secretes <strong>progesterone</strong>, which converts the endometrium to a secretory lining ready for implantation; if no embryo signals with human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG), the corpus luteum dies after about 14 days, progesterone and oestrogen fall, and the lining is shed. The luteal phase is fixed at about 14 days; it is the follicular phase that lengthens a 35-day cycle, so ovulation in such a cycle is near day 21, not day 14 — a fact that matters for natural family planning and for the woman trying to conceive. In a man LH drives the Leydig cells to make <strong>testosterone</strong> and FSH drives the Sertoli cells that support <strong>spermatogenesis</strong>, a process that takes about 70 to 75 days, so a fever or a drug affects the semen seen two to three months later. In both sexes the product switches off its own production by negative feedback, which is exactly how hormonal contraception works: steady exogenous progestogen, with or without oestrogen, keeps FSH and LH low, so no follicle ripens and there is no surge.",
