          alt: "Gynaecological problems — sort by the main complaint. Bleeding, pain, a lump, or a discharge: each points to a different short list. Heavy or irregular bleeding: fibroids, polyps, hormonal imbalance — always check haemoglobin, chronic loss causes severe anaemia quietly; bleeding after menopause is cancer until proved otherwise. Pelvic pain: sudden and severe — ovarian torsion, ruptured cyst, ectopic pregnancy; cyclical and long-standing — endometriosis, chronic infection. A lump or something coming down: prolapse — worse on standing and straining, better lying down; fibroid — firm, in the abdomen; Bartholin cyst — beside the opening. The question asked first, always: could she be pregnant? An ectopic pregnancy imitates every condition on this page and is the only one that kills within the hour. Cervical cancer is the preventable one — caused by persistent human papillomavirus infection; vaccination protects before exposure and screening finds the changes years before cancer. Ghana vaccinates girls at nine years and screens with visual inspection with acetic acid.",
          caption: "Four complaints, four short lists, and one question before all of them. Each later lesson of this course is the long version of one box on this diagram.",
