            "Position her supine with knees bent and apart and heels together (the dorsal position), or in lithotomy on a couch with supports, with a pillow under the head and a sheet over the knees. <strong>Inspect the vulva</strong> with gloved hands and a good light: the hair pattern, the labia, the clitoris (and any evidence of genital cutting, recorded by type), the urethral meatus, the introitus, the perineum and the anus — for ulcers, warts, discharge, swelling of a Bartholin's gland at the five or seven o'clock position, atrophy, excoriation, scars, bruising or tears that might indicate assault, and a bulge at the introitus on straining that suggests prolapse. For the <strong>Cusco (bivalve) speculum</strong>, warm it and lubricate the blades lightly with water or a water-based gel (not before cytology or swabs if it would contaminate the sample — use water only), part the labia with the non-dominant hand, insert the closed speculum gently at a slight angle pointing downwards and backwards along the axis of the vagina, rotate it to the horizontal, open the blades slowly until the cervix comes into view and fix them. Look at the <strong>cervix</strong>: its colour, the external os (round in the nullipara, slit-like after vaginal birth), ectropion, polyps, ulceration, friable or cauliflower-like growth that bleeds on touch, discharge from the os, the threads of an intrauterine device, and the squamocolumnar junction that VIA and cytology sample. Take the specimens in the right order — cytology or HPV sample before swabs, endocervical swab after the high vaginal swab — then loosen the blades, withdraw slowly while watching the vaginal walls, and close the speculum only once clear of the cervix so no tissue is pinched. The <strong>Sims speculum</strong>, used with the woman in the left lateral position, retracts the posterior wall so the anterior wall and any cystocele or fistula can be seen, and the woman can be asked to cough or bear down.",
            "For the <strong>bimanual examination</strong>, lubricate the gloved index and middle fingers of one hand, insert them into the vagina and place the other hand on the lower abdomen. Feel the cervix — its consistency (firm like the tip of the nose when not pregnant, soft in pregnancy), its position and whether moving it from side to side causes pain (<strong>cervical excitation</strong>, the sign of pelvic peritoneal irritation in pelvic inflammatory disease and ectopic pregnancy). Lift the uterus towards the abdominal hand to assess its size (normal is about the size of a small pear; it is described in weeks of a pregnancy-equivalent when enlarged), shape, consistency, position (anteverted in most women, retroverted in about a fifth, which is normal), mobility and tenderness; then move the fingers into each lateral fornix and press the abdominal hand down to feel the <strong>adnexae</strong> — normal ovaries are usually not felt; a tender, boggy fullness suggests an abscess or ectopic, a smooth mobile mass a cyst. Note any mass in the pouch of Douglas and fullness or tenderness there. A <strong>rectovaginal examination</strong> is added when the posterior wall, the uterosacral ligaments or a rectocele need assessing. Remove the gloves, cover her, help her sit up, and explain what you found before she dresses. A vaginal examination is <strong>not performed</strong> in a woman bleeding after 24 weeks of pregnancy until placenta praevia has been excluded by ultrasound, in a girl before puberty (examination under anaesthesia if needed), or in anyone who does not consent; it is deferred, and done only by the forensic examiner, in the woman who has been raped and has not yet been examined for evidence.",
