            "The <strong>urine pregnancy test</strong> is the first investigation in any woman of reproductive age with pelvic pain, bleeding, amenorrhoea or an unexplained collapse; it detects hCG at about 25 IU/L and is positive from around the time of the missed period, most reliably on an early-morning sample. A negative test with a strong clinical suspicion of ectopic pregnancy is repeated or followed by a serum quantitative hCG. A <strong>midstream urine</strong> is collected after cleaning the vulva front to back, discarding the first part of the stream and catching the middle, and reaches the laboratory within two hours or is refrigerated; dipstick nitrites and leucocytes suggest infection, protein suggests renal disease or pre-eclampsia, and blood may come from the tract or from menstruation, which is why the date of the LMP is written on the form. A <strong>high vaginal swab</strong> is taken from the posterior fornix under direct vision for candida, trichomonas and bacterial vaginosis; an <strong>endocervical swab</strong>, rotated in the os for ten seconds after cleaning away mucus, is for gonorrhoea and chlamydia where nucleic acid tests or culture exist; both go into the transport medium the laboratory specifies, labelled at the bedside with name, date, site and the clinical question. Vulval swabbing — for specimens as for catheterisation — is always front to back, one swab per stroke, so that organisms from the anus are not carried towards the urethra.",
